  <body>
    <sec id="sec-workflow">
      <title>Workflow</title>
      <p>This section documents how the manuscript is built and should be
  removed before submission. The source is the Quarto file
  <monospace>index.qmd</monospace>, and
  <monospace>_quarto.yml</monospace> configures a Quarto
  <italic>manuscript</italic> project that renders HTML, Word, JATS and
  a RevTeX PDF from that single source.</p>
      <sec id="building">
        <title>Building</title>
        <p>Render all formats, or only the PDF, with</p>
        <code language="bash">quarto render
quarto render index.qmd --to pdf</code>
        <p>Output is written to <monospace>_manuscript/</monospace>. Because
    <monospace>freeze: true</monospace> is set, a project-wide render
    reuses stored code output; rendering
    <monospace>index.qmd</monospace> explicitly re-executes the Python
    chunks. Use <monospace>quarto preview</monospace> for live reloading
    while writing.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="revtex-setup">
        <title>RevTeX setup</title>
        <p>The PDF uses <monospace>documentclass: revtex4-2</monospace> with
    the options
    <monospace>aps, pra, reprint, superscriptaddress</monospace>; change
    <monospace>pra</monospace> to target another journal, or
    <monospace>reprint</monospace> to <monospace>preprint</monospace>
    for a single-column draft. The folder
    <monospace>_revtex/</monospace> holds the glue between Quarto and
    RevTeX. The file <monospace>before-body.tex</monospace> emits
    <monospace>\title</monospace>, <monospace>\author</monospace>,
    <monospace>\affiliation</monospace>, <monospace>\email</monospace>
    and the abstract after <monospace>\begin{document}</monospace>, as
    RevTeX requires. The file <monospace>preamble.tex</monospace>
    resolves a <monospace>longtable*</monospace> clash with the
    <monospace>caption</monospace> package. The generated LaTeX is kept
    in <monospace>_manuscript/_tex/</monospace> for journal
    submission.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="fonts">
        <title>Fonts</title>
        <p>Text and mathematics are set in STIX Two in both the PDF and the
    HTML output. The font files are in <monospace>_fonts/</monospace>,
    and they are switched on by <monospace>stix: true</monospace> in
    <monospace>_quarto.yml</monospace>; set it to
    <monospace>false</monospace> to use the default fonts. The filter
    <monospace>_fonts/stix.lua</monospace> loads the fonts in the PDF
    preamble and, for HTML, tells MathJax 4 to use its STIX Two math
    font.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="sec-plots">
        <title>Plots</title>
        <p>The first code chunk in this file loads the local package
    <monospace>revtexplot</monospace>, which styles matplotlib figures
    to match the PDF. It is switched on by
    <monospace>plot-style: true</monospace> in
    <monospace>_quarto.yml</monospace>; set it to
    <monospace>false</monospace> for matplotlib’s defaults. Figures are
    one column wide by default and use 8 pt text, the size of
    <monospace>\footnotesize</monospace>, in STIX Two (or Computer
    Modern with <monospace>stix: false</monospace>). They are saved at
    exactly their set size, so the text in them matches the captions.
    The package provides these sizes:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><monospace>rp.COLUMN</monospace>: one column, 3.40 × 2.10 in
        (the default)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><monospace>rp.WIDE</monospace>: both columns, 7.06 × 2.35
        in</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><monospace>rp.figsize(width, aspect)</monospace>:
        <monospace>width</monospace> is
        <monospace>"column"</monospace>,
        <monospace>"wide"</monospace> or inches, and
        <monospace>aspect</monospace> is height / width</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>For example,
    <monospace>plt.subplots(figsize=rp.figsize("column", 1))</monospace>
    gives a square one-column figure.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="writing">
        <title>Writing</title>
        <p>Authors, affiliations, e-mail addresses and the abstract are set
    in the YAML header. Sections, equations and figures get labels with
    the prefixes <monospace>sec-</monospace>, <monospace>eq-</monospace>
    and <monospace>fig-</monospace>, and are referenced as
    <monospace>@sec-methods</monospace>,
    <monospace>@eq-gaussian</monospace> or
    <monospace>@fig-example</monospace>. A displayed equation is
    labelled by appending <monospace>{#eq-name}</monospace> after its
    closing <monospace>$$</monospace>. Citations use keys from
    <monospace>references.bib</monospace>,
    e.g. <monospace>[@knuth1984]</monospace>, and are formatted by
    BibTeX with the <monospace>apsrev4-2</monospace> style. Figures are
    produced by Python chunks whose <monospace>label</monospace> and
    <monospace>fig-cap</monospace> options set the reference and
    caption; see
    <xref alt="Section 4" rid="sec-results">Section 4</xref> for an
    example.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="wide-figures-and-equations">
        <title>Wide figures and equations</title>
        <p>In the two-column <monospace>reprint</monospace> layout, a figure
    spans both columns when it uses RevTeX’s
    <monospace>figure*</monospace> environment. For a Python chunk, add
    the options</p>
        <code language="python">#| fig-env: "figure*"
#| fig-pos: "t"</code>
        <p>and create the figure with
    <monospace>plt.subplots(figsize=rp.WIDE)</monospace> (see
    <xref alt="Section 1.4" rid="sec-plots">Section 1.4</xref>). For an
    image file, write</p>
        <code language="markdown">![Caption.](figures/wide.pdf){#fig-wide
  fig-env="figure*" fig-pos="t"}</code>
        <p>Wide figures float to the top of a following page. Always set
    <monospace>fig-pos: "t"</monospace>: Quarto places chunk
    figures with <monospace>[H]</monospace> by default, which makes a
    <monospace>figure*</monospace> vanish from the PDF. Do not use
    <monospace>column: page</monospace>; it loads the
    <monospace>sidenotes</monospace> package, which stops every
    <monospace>figure*</monospace> from spanning both columns.</p>
        <p>A long equation is set across both columns by wrapping it in
    RevTeX’s <monospace>widetext</monospace> environment with raw LaTeX
    blocks:</p>
        <code language="markdown">```{=latex}
\begin{widetext}
```
$$
E = \sum_n a_n x^n + \dots
$$ {#eq-wide}
```{=latex}
\end{widetext}
```</code>
        <p>Labels and cross-references work as usual. The HTML output
    ignores <monospace>fig-env</monospace>,
    <monospace>fig-pos</monospace> and the raw LaTeX, so both render
    normally there.</p>
      </sec>
    </sec>
    <sec id="introduction">
      <title>Introduction</title>
      <p>This is placeholder text for the introduction. Literate programming
  (<xref alt="Knuth 1984" rid="ref-knuth1984" ref-type="bibr">Knuth
  1984</xref>) advocates weaving code and prose into a single document,
  an idea that Quarto applies to scientific manuscripts. The typesetting
  itself is done by TeX
  (<xref alt="Knuth 1986" rid="ref-knuth1986" ref-type="bibr">Knuth
  1986</xref>) and LaTeX
  (<xref alt="Lamport 1994" rid="ref-lamport1994" ref-type="bibr">Lamport
  1994</xref>). Multiple references can be cited together
  (<xref alt="Knuth 1984" rid="ref-knuth1984" ref-type="bibr">Knuth
  1984</xref>;
  <xref alt="Lamport 1994" rid="ref-lamport1994" ref-type="bibr">Lamport
  1994</xref>).</p>
    </sec>
    <sec id="sec-methods">
      <title>Methods</title>
      <sec id="inline-and-displayed-mathematics">
        <title>Inline and displayed mathematics</title>
        <p>Inline mathematics is written between single dollar signs, as in
    <inline-formula><alternatives><tex-math><![CDATA[e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0]]></tex-math><mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline"><mml:mrow><mml:msup><mml:mi>e</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mi>i</mml:mi><mml:mi>π</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:msup><mml:mo>+</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>0</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.
    Displayed equations are written between double dollar signs and
    numbered when given a label, as for the Gaussian integral
    <styled-content id="eq-gaussian"><disp-formula><alternatives><tex-math><![CDATA[
    \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-a x^2}\, dx = \sqrt{\frac{\pi}{a}},
    \qquad a > 0.
     \qquad(1)]]></tex-math><mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mml:mrow><mml:msubsup><mml:mo>∫</mml:mo><mml:mrow><mml:mi>−</mml:mi><mml:mi>∞</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mi>∞</mml:mi></mml:msubsup><mml:msup><mml:mi>e</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mi>−</mml:mi><mml:mi>a</mml:mi><mml:msup><mml:mi>x</mml:mi><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msup></mml:mrow></mml:msup><mml:mspace width="0.167em"/><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mi>x</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:msqrt><mml:mfrac><mml:mi>π</mml:mi><mml:mi>a</mml:mi></mml:mfrac></mml:msqrt><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mspace width="2.0em"/><mml:mi>a</mml:mi><mml:mo>&gt;</mml:mo><mml:mn>0</mml:mn><mml:mi>.</mml:mi><mml:mspace width="2.0em"/><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></disp-formula></styled-content></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="multi-line-equations">
        <title>Multi-line equations</title>
        <p>Long expressions can be split with an
    <monospace>aligned</monospace> environment inside a single labelled
    equation,
    <styled-content id="eq-taylor"><disp-formula><alternatives><tex-math><![CDATA[
    \begin{aligned}
    f(x) &= \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{f^{(n)}(x_0)}{n!} (x - x_0)^n \\
         &= f(x_0) + f'(x_0)(x - x_0) + \mathcal{O}\big((x - x_0)^2\big).
    \end{aligned}
     \qquad(2)]]></tex-math><mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mml:mrow><mml:mtable><mml:mtr><mml:mtd columnalign="right" style="text-align: right; padding-right: 0"><mml:mi>f</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>x</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mtd><mml:mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left; padding-left: 0"><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:munderover><mml:mo>∑</mml:mo><mml:mrow><mml:mi>n</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>0</mml:mn></mml:mrow><mml:mo accent="false">∞</mml:mo></mml:munderover><mml:mfrac><mml:mrow><mml:msup><mml:mi>f</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>n</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>x</mml:mi><mml:mn>0</mml:mn></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mi>n</mml:mi><mml:mi>!</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:mfrac><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>x</mml:mi><mml:mo>−</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>x</mml:mi><mml:mn>0</mml:mn></mml:msub><mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mi>n</mml:mi></mml:msup></mml:mtd></mml:mtr><mml:mtr><mml:mtd columnalign="right" style="text-align: right; padding-right: 0"/><mml:mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left; padding-left: 0"><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mi>f</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>x</mml:mi><mml:mn>0</mml:mn></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>+</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mi>f</mml:mi><mml:mo>′</mml:mo></mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>x</mml:mi><mml:mn>0</mml:mn></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>x</mml:mi><mml:mo>−</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>x</mml:mi><mml:mn>0</mml:mn></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>+</mml:mo><mml:mi>𝒪</mml:mi><mml:mo minsize="120%" maxsize="120%" stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>x</mml:mi><mml:mo>−</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>x</mml:mi><mml:mn>0</mml:mn></mml:msub><mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msup><mml:mo minsize="120%" maxsize="120%" stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mi>.</mml:mi></mml:mtd></mml:mtr></mml:mtable><mml:mspace width="2.0em"/><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></disp-formula></styled-content></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="cross-references">
        <title>Cross-references</title>
        <p>Equations are referenced as
    <xref alt="Equation 1" rid="eq-gaussian">Equation 1</xref> and
    <xref alt="Equation 2" rid="eq-taylor">Equation 2</xref>, sections
    as <xref alt="Section 4" rid="sec-results">Section 4</xref>, and
    figures as <xref alt="Figure 1" rid="fig-example">Figure 1</xref>.
    The numbers are assigned automatically in every output format.</p>
      </sec>
    </sec>
    <sec id="sec-results">
      <title>Results</title>
      <p><xref alt="Figure 1" rid="fig-example">Figure 1</xref> is generated
  by the Python code chunk embedded in the source file. It shows the
  damped oscillation
  <styled-content id="eq-damped"><disp-formula><alternatives><tex-math><![CDATA[
  f(t) = e^{-\gamma t} \cos(\omega t).
   \qquad(3)]]></tex-math><mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>f</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>t</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mi>e</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mi>−</mml:mi><mml:mi>γ</mml:mi><mml:mi>t</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:msup><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">cos</mml:mi><mml:mo>⁡</mml:mo></mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>ω</mml:mi><mml:mi>t</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mi>.</mml:mi><mml:mspace width="2.0em"/><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></disp-formula></styled-content>
  for three values of the damping rate <inline-formula><alternatives><tex-math><![CDATA[\gamma]]></tex-math><mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline"><mml:mi>γ</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.</p>
      <fig id="fig-example">
        <caption>
          <p>Figure 1: Damped oscillations for three values of the
    damping rate <inline-formula><alternatives><tex-math><![CDATA[\gamma]]></tex-math><mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline"><mml:mi>γ</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
    with <inline-formula><alternatives><tex-math><![CDATA[\omega = 2\pi]]></tex-math><mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>ω</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mi>π</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.</p>
        </caption>
        <graphic mimetype="image" mime-subtype="png" xlink:href="index_files/figure-jats/fig-example-output-1.png"/>
      </fig>
      <p><xref alt="Figure 2" rid="fig-wide">Figure 2</xref> spans both
  columns and shows each damping rate in its own panel.</p>
      <fig id="fig-wide">
        <caption>
          <p>Figure 2: The oscillations of
    <xref alt="Figure 1" rid="fig-example">Figure 1</xref> with their
    envelopes <inline-formula><alternatives><tex-math><![CDATA[\pm e^{-\gamma t}]]></tex-math><mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>±</mml:mi><mml:msup><mml:mi>e</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mi>−</mml:mi><mml:mi>γ</mml:mi><mml:mi>t</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:msup></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    (dashed).</p>
        </caption>
        <graphic mimetype="image" mime-subtype="png" xlink:href="index_files/figure-jats/fig-wide-output-1.png"/>
      </fig>
    </sec>
